  "summary": "Palm-stick maps of ocean swell interference — navigation knowledge lashed in wood and cowrie.",
  "description": "Marshallese navigators lashed midribs of coconut fronds into rebbelib: stick charts where curved sticks represent how ocean swells bend, meet and interfere around atolls, and cowrie shells mark island positions. Far from maps of land, they diagram the feel of the sea — charts trained into navigator-paddlers who read swell patterns against hull and body. Two training types, mattang and meddo, taught single-island and regional swell logic. The charts never went to sea; they stayed ashore as mnemonic sculpture for an oral navigation school unique to the Marshalls.",
